    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the whole suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented idempotent owner creation in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`), the controller behind `POST /api/owners`.\n\n**Behavior:** When a create request carries an `Idempotency-Key` header whose value has already been used for a successful create, the endpoint returns the originally created owner with **200 OK** instead of running the create path again (which would otherwise reject the repeat as a duplicate with 409). A first-time key creates normally and returns **201 Created**.\n\n**How it works:**\n- Added a `ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> idempotentCreates` field mapping each seen key \u2192 created owner id.\n- At the top of `addOwner`, the key is looked up; if a mapped owner still exists, its DTO is returned with 200.\n- After a successful `saveOwner`, the key \u2192 new owner id is recorded.\n- A small `idempotencyKey()` helper reads the `Idempotency-Key` header via `RequestContextHolder`/`ServletRequestAttributes`, returning `null` when the header is absent/blank. This deliberately avoids changing the OpenAPI-generated `OwnersApi.addOwner` signature, so the `@Override` and request mapping stay intact.\n\nThe idempotency check short-circuits before validation/duplicate logic, so a repeated create with a seen key never re-runs those rules and never creates a second record.",
